1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,337
From ingot to wafer.

2
00:00:02,287 --> 00:00:05,887
This is episode two of From Sand to GPU.

3
00:00:05,887 --> 00:00:12,075
Episode one ended with electronic-grade polysilicon — ultra-pure feedstock that still is not a chip.

4
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:20,375
In this episode, that feedstock becomes a single crystal, then a stack of mirror-flat disks that a foundry can actually print transistors on.

5
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:26,362
Electronic-grade polysilicon is melted in a fused-quartz crucible made from high-purity quartz.

6
00:00:26,362 --> 00:00:33,625
A seed crystal dips into the melt, and the Czochralski process slowly pulls a single-crystal ingot, sometimes called a boule.

7
00:00:33,625 --> 00:00:39,375
Dopants such as boron or phosphorus set the substrate resistivity the device designers ordered.

8
00:00:39,375 --> 00:00:44,237
This is where crystal quality is born — or where latent defects are locked in.

9
00:00:44,237 --> 00:00:49,550
The ingot is cropped, ground, and marked with a notch or flat for crystal orientation.

10
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:53,312
Then a multi-wire saw slices it into raw wafers.

11
00:00:53,312 --> 00:00:55,825
Those slices are still rough.

12
00:00:55,825 --> 00:00:59,550
Lapping, etching, and precision polishing follow.

13
00:00:59,550 --> 00:01:09,362
Many logic wafers also receive an epitaxial layer — a thin, engineered crystal skin grown on the polished surface to improve the starting material for advanced devices.

14
00:01:09,362 --> 00:01:14,975
Leading-edge AI GPU logic is overwhelmingly built on three-hundred-millimeter wafers.

15
00:01:14,975 --> 00:01:19,262
That is the workhorse diameter for modern foundry capacity.

16
00:01:19,262 --> 00:01:22,487
Two-hundred-millimeter wafers still matter.

17
00:01:22,487 --> 00:01:34,762
Power-management ICs, analog parts, and specialty chips often ride two-hundred-millimeter lines — and they still show up on the same system board as a GPU, even when the compute die itself is three-hundred-millimeter.

18
00:01:34,762 --> 00:01:38,225
Silicon wafer supply is an oligopoly.

19
00:01:38,225 --> 00:01:45,450
The names that dominate industry maps include Shin-Etsu, SUMCO, GlobalWafers, Siltronic, and SK Siltron.

20
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:53,900
Exact share percentages move year to year, so treat market-share figures as estimates unless they come from primary SEMI tables or company filings.

21
00:01:53,900 --> 00:02:00,262
The confirmed risk is concentration itself: a few producers, heavy CapEx, and long lead times.

22
00:02:00,262 --> 00:02:08,187
Wafer makers obsess over crystal defects, oxygen control, flatness, nanotopography, particles, and resistivity.

23
00:02:08,187 --> 00:02:12,362
A wafer that fails prime specs never becomes a leading-edge die.

24
00:02:12,362 --> 00:02:17,375
Expanding three-hundred-millimeter capacity takes years and enormous capital.

25
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:21,837
It is not a short-cycle response when AI demand spikes overnight.

26
00:02:21,837 --> 00:02:27,987
For a modern AI accelerator, three-hundred-millimeter wafers feed more than the GPU logic die.

27
00:02:27,987 --> 00:02:31,325
Leading HBM DRAM is also wafer-based.

28
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:37,062
In some CoWoS-S packaging flows, additional silicon is consumed as an interposer.

29
00:02:37,062 --> 00:02:42,762
Silicon intensity per shipped GPU package is higher than a single-chip story suggests.

30
00:02:42,762 --> 00:02:46,612
What should a supply-chain tracker watch after this episode?

31
00:02:46,612 --> 00:02:49,775
SEMI silicon wafer shipment trends.

32
00:02:49,775 --> 00:02:52,825
Wafer pricing and long-term agreements.

33
00:02:52,825 --> 00:02:59,212
Geographic and energy risk near major wafer fabs, including concentrated Japanese capacity.

34
00:02:59,212 --> 00:03:03,250
And epitaxial capacity earmarked for advanced logic.

35
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:09,587
That is episode two: from electronic-grade melt to polished wafers — the bridge from chemistry to the fab.

36
00:03:09,587 --> 00:03:16,900
Next time: inside the front-end fab, where lithography, etch, and deposition turn a blank wafer into a logic die.

